WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.152 --> 00:00:02.740
(hip-hop beat)

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.450
- The majority of licensed
medical physicians

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:08.510
practicing medicine in
the US today are MDs,

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.510
also known as "medical doctors."

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.500
However, there is also
an increasing number

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.130
of practicing physicians known as DOs,

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:17.580
which means "doctors of
osteopathic medicine."

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.960
Both are equally licensed
to practice medicine

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.370
in all 50 states.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:24.110
I wanna make one point
very clear before we start:

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.570
one degree is not better than the other.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.600
In fact, they have so many similarities

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.370
it would probably take
an hour to cover them.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:33.120
This video is gonna focus
only on the differences

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.280
between an MD and a DO

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.670
The most evident
difference between the two

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.930
is that DOs practice
something called O.M.T.,

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:43.160
which is "osteopathic
manipulative therapy."

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.650
Instead of just telling you,
I'd much rather show you.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:46.483
Come on.

00:00:46.483 --> 00:00:49.840
Osteopathic manipulation is
a hands-on therapeutic tool

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.820
whereby manipulating
the body's own tissues

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.810
can restore and maintain optimal function.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.490
And it's a low-risk, conservative measure

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.320
that can be used in place
of medication and surgery.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.190
And it treats all types of ailments,

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.810
everything from low back pain,

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.890
to something more complex
like congestive heart failure.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.090
Take a deep breath in.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:09.923
Deep breath out.

00:01:10.808 --> 00:01:11.807
(neck popping)

00:01:11.807 --> 00:01:12.670
(patient laughing)

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:13.590
How's that feel?

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:14.423
- Really good!

00:01:14.423 --> 00:01:16.960
- In osteopathic medical
school, there's a heavy emphasis

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.360
placed on the holistic medical approach.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:20.540
Now what does that mean?

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.620
It means an extra focus on the body's

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.480
own ability to heal itself,

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.940
a focus on prevention, and
a focus on the whole patient

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:29.840
instead of just the disease process.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.680
Just because there's a heavier emphasis

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.720
placed on this holistic
model in osteopathic school,

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.680
it doesn't mean that MDs don't practice

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:37.690
with this model as well.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.440
There are plenty of MDs who treat

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.560
with the whole patient philosophy in mind.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.100
Those first two differences
were just differences

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.580
in medical school training.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.410
Now let's move on to
residency, which is arguably

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.400
the most important part
of medical training.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.870
In order for a residency program

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.360
to be considered legitimate,

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.250
it needs to be authorized
by an accreditation council,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.670
and currently there are
two accreditation councils:

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.990
one for MDs and one for DOs.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.560
DOs can train under either
accreditation council,

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.150
however MDs can only
train under the MD banner.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.590
Come 2020, those two
accreditation councils will merge,

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.520
and allow residents to train
under a unified council,

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.010
further strengthening my
point that there're becoming

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.360
less and less differences
between the two degrees.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.930
Now, how about practicing internationally?

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.350
Well, here, MDs have a distinct advantage,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.810
as they're able to practice medicine

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.350
in more countries than DOs.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:29.010
Currently about 50 countries
allow full medical licensing

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.190
for US-trained DOs, with
more countries allowing

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.680
restricted practicing as well.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.800
This restriction stems from the confusion

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.370
created by European-trained osteopaths,

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.560
who aren't medical doctors,
but are only trained

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.840
in manipulation of the
musculoskeletal system.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.530
However, the AOA has
been making great strides

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.200
in educating foreign governments

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.510
about US-trained osteopathic doctors.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:51.870
Now what about the applicants?

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.980
To get into an MD or DO program
is extremely competitive,

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.700
with the number of applicants
and score requirements

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.700
rising each year.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.320
It's important to know
that DO schools do accept

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.770
the non-traditional
medical school applicant:

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:05.930
those who are going into medicine

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.060
as their second career,
or perhaps later in life,

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.900
or don't have a scientific background.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.280
Also the GPA/MCAT combo
required for admission

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.230
is slightly lower for DO schools,

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.240
but that gap is closing each year.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.280
Interestingly, because
there are less DO schools,

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.940
despite the lower requirements,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.460
it's still more competitive
to get into a DO school.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:26.840
A couple more differences.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.130
There's a 9:1 ratio of
MDs to DOs out there,

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.720
with about 100,000 DOs in active practice.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.250
And two, a higher percentage of DOs

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.980
go into primary care specialties

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.230
like family medicine,
OBGYN, or pediatrics.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.950
Me personally, I chose the DO route,

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.680
because I'm a fan of treating
the patient as a whole

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.350
and the prevention aspect.

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.660
Plus, it never hurts to have
a hands-on tool like OMT.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.970
Bottom line is, when
you're picking your doctor,

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:55.030
you should focus less on their degree,

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.020
and more on their communication skills,

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.273
knowledge, bedside manner, and experience.

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:03.356
(jazzy hip-hop beat)

